# Alo King Driver Operations and Dispatch Strategy

## Executive recommendation

Alo King should **not** use a permanent “one order per driver” rule, and it should **not** allow drivers to freely pick up a second order simply because it appears nearby.

Use a controlled model:

> **One active delivery by default. Add a second order only when the dispatch engine proves that the extra pickup/drop does not materially damage food quality, promised ETA, route safety, or cash control.**

For Lebanon’s Shouf terrain, the best starting model is:

- **one order per driver** for long-distance, fragile, high-value, delayed, or difficult mountain routes;
- **two-order batching** only for the same restaurant or a compatible pickup corridor;
- no more than **two active customer drops** in the initial pilot;
- no driver self-selecting extra orders without dispatch authorization;
- cars/4WDs for cross-valley and steep routes, scooters for short local ridge routes;
- dispatch based on real road time, elevation, road constraints, food readiness, and driver capacity—not straight-line distance.

