# 6. Driver compensation and incentives

## 6.1 Compensation model

Use transparent pay, not opaque algorithmic payouts.

Recommended structure:

```text
base pickup fee
+ distance/zone component
+ terrain component
+ waiting component
+ second-stop component
+ weather/late-night premium
+ approved return/exception fee
```

Batching should never reduce the driver’s pay compared with two fair single-order assignments. It can provide an efficiency bonus, but it should not transfer all savings to Alo King.

## 6.2 Waiting pay

Pay waiting compensation when:

- the driver arrived within the assigned pickup window;
- the restaurant was not ready;
- the wait exceeded the grace period;
- the driver did not cause the delay.

This gives restaurants and dispatch a real incentive to provide accurate ready times.

## 6.3 Driver scorecard

Track:

- on-time pickup;
- on-time delivery;
- cancellations after acceptance;
- customer complaints;
- missing/wrong order incidents;
- cash discrepancies;
- GPS availability;
- safe driving incidents;
- acceptance rate, but do not punish legitimate declines excessively;
- batching quality;
- customer ratings.

Do not optimize only for acceptance rate. A driver who declines an unsafe or impossible route may be making the correct operational decision.

## 6.4 Fairness and safety

Drivers must be able to decline:

- unsafe roads;
- severe weather;
- overloaded routes;
- suspicious or abusive requests;
- routes beyond vehicle capability;
- cash exposure above their limit.

A safety decline should not reduce a driver’s score in the same way as an avoidable decline.

